Tasks
1. Set up the Ansible workspace on the control node

Create a dedicated working directory named "workspace" under /home/user.

All lab files must be created and managed from this directory.

2. Define the managed node in inventory.ini

Inside the workspace directory, create an inventory.ini file that defines a host group named web.

The inventory must include:

The managed node server1

Correct SSH authentication details

Privilege escalation credentials so Ansible can perform administrative tasks

This step ensures Ansible can:

Connect to server1 over SSH

Run tasks with elevated privileges (become)

Target the correct host group during playbook execution

3. Create a Jinja2 template for NGINX configuration

Create a Jinja2 template file named nginx.conf.j2 in the workspace.

The template should:

Represent an NGINX server block configuration

Use Jinja2 variable placeholders instead of hardcoded values

Reference variables for:

The listening port

The server name

The web root directory

This task introduces template-based configuration management, where the same template can be reused across environments by changing variables instead of rewriting files.

4. Define variables inside the playbook

In your Ansible playbook, define variables that will be injected into the Jinja2 template at runtime.

These variables should represent:

The port on which NGINX listens (use the default port commonly used by NGINX for HTTP traffic)

The server name (configure it for local access during development and testing)

The document root for the website (use the standard web root directory used by NGINX on most Linux systems: /var/www/html)

Defining variables in the playbook demonstrates how Ansible separates configuration logic (templates) from environment-specific values (variables).

5. Create the Ansible playbook to deploy the template

Create a playbook named "nginx-template.yml" inside the workspace directory.

The playbook must:

Target the web host group

Use privilege escalation (become)

Disable fact gathering for faster execution

Ensure the NGINX package is installed on the managed node

Deploy the rendered Jinja2 template to /etc/nginx/nginx.conf

This step ensures:

Required infrastructure (NGINX) exists before configuration is applied

The template is rendered safely and idempotently

Configuration deployment follows production best practices

6. Execute the playbook on the managed node

Run the playbook against server1 using the inventory file.

During execution, Ansible should:

Install NGINX if it is not already present

Render the Jinja2 template with the defined variables

Copy the final configuration file to /etc/nginx/nginx.conf

7. Verify the rendered configuration on server1

After execution, verify that:

The file /etc/nginx/nginx.conf exists on server1

Variable placeholders have been replaced with actual values

The final configuration matches the expected output

This confirms successful template rendering and variable substitution, which is the core learning objective of this lab.

Outcomes
After completing this lab, you will be able to:

Understand how Ansible variables work

Use Jinja2 templates (.j2) with Ansible

Safely deploy configuration files using the template module

Handle dependencies correctly in playbooks

Validate and verify configuration changes on remote servers


